  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nThere is an increasing chance for scattered storms this evening\nas a weak cold front travels eastward out of the Dakotas. \nLatest satellite imagery shows a somewhat slowly deteriorating \nlow level cloud deck across western MN while high-level anvil \nclouds overspread from the southwest. In the areas that have \nseen enough clearing, increasing dewpoints and warming \ntemperatures have built in a pool of moderately unstable air. \nThis destabilization combined with the weak forcing from the \nfront are expected to support the development of widely \nscattered storms this evening. Most CAMs have aligned on this \nsolution, with some more bullish than others. Confidence is low \nthat everyone will see storms tonight, but the trend supports at\nleast a broader coverage. Given the limited heating across \neastern MN, we expect any storms that develop out west to wane \nthe further east they get. Nothing severe is expected given the \nweak midlevel lapse rates and wind shear, though some strong \ngusts could be possible as the storms first develop across \nwestern MN.\n\nAs the front exits overnight, winds increase out of the\nnorthwest and bring in much drier air. While it may be breezy \nat times with gusts up to 20 mph, Sunday will be a very nice day\nwith plenty of sunshine and dewpoints in the 50s.\n\nActive weather returns Monday into Tuesday as an upper level  \nshortwave deepens over the Hudson Bay. For the most part, Monday \nwill remain nice with mostly clear skies. Increasing southwest \nwinds will begin to advect moisture in though, resulting a \nslightly more humid day and a chance for late afternoon storms.\nOur latest PoPs have increased in the latest forecast package \nto match this trend. The better forcing for storm development \ncomes late Monday into Tuesday as a southwestward-advancing \ncold front collides with the moderately unstable air. Depending \non the timing of the front, the better chance for any severe \nweather potential may end up favoring Iowa rather than across \nour region. You can see this trend with the latest QPF as well, \nputting the bullseye more into northern Iowa rather than \nsouthern MN as it looked 24 hours ago.\n\nBehind this front, temperatures cool off slightly on Wednesday,\nthen gradually recover through the rest of the week.",
